# ===========================================================================
# STUDIO FRAGMENT — NAMESPACE PACK (BATCH INFERENCE)
# Rendered by studio/backend into tenants/$$project/rendered/namespace-pack.yaml
# ===========================================================================
# The HOST-side ground for a batch run. Structurally the same pack every other
# template writes — namespace, ceiling, defaults, storage — with two differences,
# both of which come from the same fact: a run has a file going IN and a file
# coming OUT, and nothing staying up.
#
#   1. TWO PVCs, not three. There is no vector index. `scratch` is 5Gi rather
#      than 1Gi because it holds the dataset AND the results.
#   2. hive.ksl/template: studio-batch, so Studio can tell what it owns.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE EXPIRY ANNOTATION IS WRITTEN TWICE, AND THIS IS THE FIRST TIME
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A service sets `hive.ksl/expiry` once, from `durationDays`, and never moves it.
# A run cannot do that: the clock a PI chose is `artifactRetentionDays`, and it
# only starts when there are artifacts to retain — which is not known until the
# work finishes.
#
# So this writes a SAFETY NET: provisioning time plus the retention period,
# which is always EARLIER than the final value and therefore never leaves a
# project alive longer than intended if the run dies before `collect-results`
# can set the real one. A namespace with no expiry at all would be the failure
# worth avoiding, because the reconciler would never collect it.
#
# `collect-results` PATCHes it to completion time plus retention once the run
# has actually produced something. Studio only ever SETS this annotation; the
# host reconciler CronJob is what honours it (CLAUDE.md §2.6).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TEMPLATING CONTRACT — READ BEFORE EDITING
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two passes, in this order:
#
#   PASS 1 — CONDITIONAL BLOCKS. Lines between `#@if <key>=<value>` and `#@end`
#     are KEPT only when the render facts contain that key/value pair. Marker
#     lines are always removed. Blocks do not nest.
#     Facts for this fragment:  tier = small | medium
#
#   PASS 2 — string.Template SUBSTITUTION over what pass 1 left. Placeholders
#     are $$name / $${name}. A LITERAL dollar sign must be written `$$`.
#
# Placeholders used here:
#   $$project      the PI's project name, validated SERVER-SIDE against
#                 ^[a-z0-9-]{3,20}$$ and the reserved list
#   $$tier         small | medium
#   $$ttl_expiry   RFC3339 UTC — provisioning time + artifactRetentionDays
# ===========================================================================
apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: p-$project
  labels:
    hive.ksl/project: $project
    hive.ksl/tier: $tier
    # THE OWNERSHIP LABEL. Studio checks this before it writes to or deletes
    # anything, and it is why a batch project can never be confused with
    # p-inf-101 (t-ao) or p-inf-103 (t-sz). CLAUDE.md invariant (d).
    hive.ksl/template: studio-batch
    hive.ksl/kind: tenant
  annotations:
    # The safety net described in the header — replaced by collect-results with
    # completion time + retention once the run has produced something.
    hive.ksl/expiry: "$ttl_expiry"
    hive.ksl/lifecycle-kind: run
---
# ===========================================================================
# THE CEILING — profile `batch`
# ===========================================================================
# Enforced at ADMISSION TIME (CLAUDE.md §2.4): the API server refuses an
# over-quota pod outright. Nothing over-quota ever runs, not even briefly.
#
# THE GPU LINE IS WHAT MAKES A BATCH RUN A REAL TENANT WORKLOAD. The run's GPU
# is requested by the serving runtime running as a NATIVE SIDECAR, and a native
# sidecar's requests are counted against this quota exactly like an ordinary
# container's — VERIFIED on this cluster, observed going 0 -> 1 -> 0 across a
# run, not assumed from the docs. So a batch run is subject to the same ceiling,
# at the same admission point, as a served endpoint, and two runs in one project
# serialise behind it rather than both starting.
#
# STORAGE IS THE NUMBER THAT MOVED, and here is the whole arithmetic:
#
#     models     1Gi   the chosen model, staged once by the seed Job
#     scratch    5Gi   the dataset going in AND the results coming out
#     vCluster   5Gi   the control plane's own etcd claim, provisioned by its
#                      StatefulSet INTO THIS NAMESPACE and counted here
#     ----------------
#     total     11Gi   ...which is why the ceiling is not 10Gi
#
# The task specified 10Gi. That is one gigabyte short of what this template's
# own three claims require, and a quota that cannot admit the resources the
# template itself creates is a quota that fails at `vcluster` every time. 12Gi
# is the smallest honest number: it matches what inference-endpoint and the RAG
# stack already use, and leaves 1Gi of headroom rather than -1Gi.
#@if tier=small
apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: tier-quota
  namespace: p-$project
  labels:
    hive.ksl/project: $project
    hive.ksl/tier: small
    hive.ksl/quota-profile: batch
spec:
  hard:
    # !! 2, NOT 1, AND THE REASON MATTERS !!
    #
    # The Small tier is ONE GPU. This quota is 2 because Knative's validating
    # webhook DRY-RUN CREATES a sample pod to validate every Revision template.
    # A batch run creates no Revision — but the tenant's namespace may also hold
    # a served endpoint, and the quota is the namespace's, not the workload's.
    # Keeping the number identical across templates means a PI who runs a batch
    # job in a project that also serves a model does not hit a ceiling that
    # moved under them. Full reasoning in DEVIATIONS.md.
    requests.nvidia.com/gpu: "2"
    limits.cpu: "8"
    limits.memory: 16Gi
    # See the arithmetic in the header block above.
    requests.storage: 12Gi
    persistentvolumeclaims: "6"
    # A batch project creates no InferenceService at all. The line is kept so
    # the ceiling is the same shape in every template — a PI is not offered a
    # different set of limits because of which card they clicked.
    count/inferenceservices.serving.kserve.io: "2"
#@end
#@if tier=medium
apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: tier-quota
  namespace: p-$project
  labels:
    hive.ksl/project: $project
    hive.ksl/tier: medium
    hive.ksl/quota-profile: batch
spec:
  hard:
    # IDENTICAL TO SMALL TODAY, AND THAT IS EXPECTED, NOT AN OVERSIGHT. Locally
    # both tiers are 1 GPU (CLAUDE.md §1), so the only honest local difference
    # is the label. Written out separately anyway so the production numbers can
    # be dropped in without restructuring, and so a diff shows which tier moved.
    requests.nvidia.com/gpu: "2"
    limits.cpu: "8"
    limits.memory: 16Gi
    requests.storage: 12Gi
    persistentvolumeclaims: "6"
    count/inferenceservices.serving.kserve.io: "2"
#@end
---
# ===========================================================================
# THE DEFAULTS THAT MAKE THE CEILING SURVIVABLE — MANDATORY, NOT TIDY
# ===========================================================================
# Once the quota sets limits.cpu and limits.memory, Kubernetes REQUIRES every
# container in this namespace to state both, and rejects any that does not:
#
#   Error from server (Forbidden): pods "x" is forbidden: failed quota:
#   tier-quota: must specify limits.cpu for: <container>
#
# So the rule is: quota on limits.* -> you MUST also ship a LimitRange.
#
# Without it, everything the PI did not write is refused: the vCluster control
# plane, the seed Job, and the staging pod in this same template.
#
# THE BATCH JOB DELIBERATELY DOES NOT RELY ON IT. Both of its containers state
# their own cpu/memory, because the default ceiling below (512Mi) would
# OOM-kill a serving runtime loading a model — a failure that surfaces as an
# unexplained Exit 137 rather than as anything about limits.
#
# NOTE this applies ONLY to cpu and memory. Extended resources such as
# nvidia.com/gpu are never defaulted, so a pod gets a GPU only by asking for one
# explicitly (CLAUDE.md §2.3) — which is exactly the behaviour wanted.
apiVersion: v1
kind: LimitRange
metadata:
  name: defaults
  namespace: p-$project
  labels:
    hive.ksl/project: $project
spec:
  limits:
    - type: Container
      defaultRequest:
        cpu: 100m
        memory: 128Mi
      default:
        cpu: 500m
        memory: 512Mi
---
# ===========================================================================
# STORAGE — TWO CLAIMS
# ===========================================================================
# models   written once by the seed Job, mounted READ-ONLY by everything after.
# scratch  the dataset going in under input/, the results coming out under
#          output/. The only claim anything writes to during the run.
#
# storageClassName `standard` is kind's local-path provisioner (production:
# WekaFS CSI with directory quotas — DEVIATIONS.md), and two consequences bite:
#
#   * volumeBindingMode is WaitForFirstConsumer, so a claim stays **Pending
#     until a pod mounts it**. A Pending PVC here is NOT a fault and the
#     provisioning UI must not report it as one.
#   * The volume is a directory on ONE node's disk, so ReadWriteOnce is the only
#     honest access mode, and each claim is pinned to whichever node FIRST
#     mounts it.
#
#     THAT PINNING DOES NOT ARRANGE ITSELF. The seed Job pins `models`. A
#     staging pod mounting only `scratch` would be free to land on the other
#     worker and pin `scratch` there — and the batch Job mounts BOTH, so no
#     single node could satisfy it:
#
#       0/3 nodes are available: 1 node(s) had untolerated taint(s),
#       2 node(s) didn't match PersistentVolume's node affinity.
#
#     The pod stays Pending for ever and the step times out with an EMPTY log,
#     because nothing ever ran. It is a coin toss, so it works until it does not.
#     staging-pod.yaml.tmpl therefore mounts `models` read-only purely as a
#     scheduling fence. This is the same trap the RAG stack hit for real.
#
#     ALL OF THIS IS A LOCAL-PATH ARTEFACT. On WekaFS (ReadWriteMany) nothing is
#     pinned to a node and the fence comes out.
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: models
  namespace: p-$project
  labels:
    hive.ksl/project: $project
    hive.ksl/role: models
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  storageClassName: standard
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 1Gi
---
# 5Gi, not 1Gi. The upload ceiling is 200MB and the results of a chat run are
# roughly the size of the input again; 5Gi holds both many times over and still
# leaves the 12Gi namespace ceiling a gigabyte of headroom.
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: scratch
  namespace: p-$project
  labels:
    hive.ksl/project: $project
    hive.ksl/role: scratch
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  storageClassName: standard
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 5Gi
